bbqX0kbd: make the BlackBerry key a Ctrl

This keyboard has no Ctrl. Reading its capability bitmap back off the device,
the only modifiers it reports are KEY_LEFTSHIFT, KEY_RIGHTSHIFT, KEY_LEFTALT
and KEY_RIGHTALT - neither KEY_LEFTCTRL nor KEY_RIGHTCTRL is advertised at all.
Alt and Sym are the two Alt codes, and they are spent on the symbols printed on
the key faces, so neither is free to become one. The result is that no keyboard
shortcut - Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+A - can be typed on this device, however well
the rest of the stack handles them.

The BlackBerry logo key is the one key that can be given up. The shell maps
Key_Menu and Key_HomePage to the same action:

    d[Qt.Key_HomePage] = "shell.launcher";
    d[Qt.Key_Menu]     = "shell.launcher";

so on this device it is a duplicate of the key directly beside it, and nothing
is lost by repurposing it. It is also the right key for the job ergonomically:
it sits under the thumb, next to the letters the shortcuts use.

No other change is needed. The driver already carries KEY_LEFTCTRL through
bbqX0kbd_modkeys_to_bits() and has a case for it in both report paths, which
tracks the modifier bit and falls through to report the key - that case was
simply unreachable while no scancode produced it. The keybits are declared from
this table (__set_bit(keycode[i], input->keybit)), so the new code declares
itself, unlike KEY_EMOJI_PICKER which is not in the table and needed its own
__set_bit. q20_report_key() swallows only the two Alt codes, so Ctrl is
reported normally; bbqX0kbd_get_altgr_keycode() passes it through its default
arm unchanged; and bbqX0kbd_set_brightness() switches only on letter keys.

Qt needs nothing either: KEY_LEFTCTRL is in its own generated keymap rather
than the webOS overlay. Verified by injecting code 29 from a uinput keyboard -
the input method logged the key as Qt::Key_Control with ControlModifier set,
and Ctrl+A/Ctrl+C/Ctrl+V reached a web application as real DOM key events.
Upstream-Status: Pending

